 { In the endgame, the team of rook and pawn often outperforms two minor pieces, a subject that we touched upon at the end of Chapter Seven. In the above position Black's task is further complicated by the strong passed pawn on b4, supported by White's active pieces. Moreover, the g6-pawn is weak. } 1. { -KEY- } b5 { The most straightforward choice. } ( { In the event of } 1. Ke5 { , Black could reply } Nh5 2. Rb7 Ke8 3. Kd6 Ba4 { and the bishop stays on the a4-e8 diagonal, ready to sacrifice itself for the b-pawn, just like in the game. Attempts to chase it away make no sense; e.g. } 4. Ra7 Bb5 5. Ra5 Bf1 { and the bishop switches to an even longer diagonal. } ) 
Bxb5 $1 { The most practical, and perhaps only solution. } ( { In contrast, after } 1... Ne4 $6 2. Rb8 { followed by b5-b6, the position remains very unpleasant for Black. If he sends his king to the queenside, he probably loses the g6-pawn, after which the h-pawn turns into a new concern for him. It is not really necessary to investigate whether Black can save a draw here - you will soon see that the kind of static inferiority faced by him in the game is much easier to deal with. } ) 
2. Rxb5 Kg7 3. Ke5 Nh5 $1 { The idea behind this fortress is simple. The only way for White to make progress is to try to win the g6-pawn. In the process, both his king and rook should attack it, which means that the f4-pawn becomes undefended. The latter fact is exploited by the knight which, in the case of capturing on f4, also protects the g6-pawn. } 4. Rb4 Kh7 5. Ke6 Kg7 6. Ra4 Ng3 ( { At this point, Psakhis continued } 6... Ng3 7. Ra8 { and after } Nh5 8. Ke5 Ng3 { a draw was agreed. } ) 
 { Let's see a more critical continuation: } 7. Ra7+ Kh6 8. Kf7 Nh5 9. Kg8 { The best try. } ( { Both } 9. Ra8 { and } 9. Ra4 { would be met by } Kh7 { when White hasn't achieved anything. Note that thanks to his spare move with the king, Black never ends up in zugzwang. } ) 
Nf6+ 10. Kh8 { At this moment, the knight can't move due to the mate on h7, so the king must leave the fortress. Fortunately, the pawns on h4 and f4 are too exposed for White to aspire to anything real. } Kh5 11. Kg7 Nd5 12. Ra4 Ne3 ( { Another solution is } 12... Kxh4 13. Rd4 ( { or } 12... Ne3 12. Kxg6 Kg4 ) 
Nxf4 $1 14. Rxf4 Kg3 { and the advance of the f-pawn secures the draw. } ) 
13. Ra6 Nd5 $1 14. Rxg6 Kxh4 15. Rg5 Nxf4 { Draw. } 
 *

