 { In Psakhis-Smyslov we saw the creation of a fortress as a good defensive option in practice; here are two further examples on this theme.Black is a whole exchange down, but White's bad kingside structure provides some slight compensation. Around this point I started working on a fortress, convinced that it was the best defensive idea available. } 1... { -KEY- } a5 $1 { To have any chance of salvation Black needs to swap the queenside pawns, thus reducing the number of possible targets in his camp, or enemy fronts, if you prefer. With the battle limited to a single flank and just one weak pawn on f7, the fortress will be more viable. } 2. bxa5 Qxa5 3. Rb1 ( { White couldn't prevent the second exchange of pawns; e.g. } 3. Qe3 b4 $1 ) 
Qxa3 4. Rxb5 Qc1 5. Qe3 Qc6 ( { a) Of course not } 5... Qxe3 $2 6. fxe3 { and White's pawn structure has been considerably improved, after which the plan of f3-f4, e3-e4 and f4-f5 in order to split the black pawns, should suffice for victory. } ) 
 ( { b) Correct was } 5... Bh6 $1 6. Qxc1 { else 6... Qg5+ is annoying for White. } Bxc1 { . We will reach a similar position later on in the game, but with the white h-pawn on h4. Here that pawn is not exposed to the bishop, so White's winning chances could be rated as somewhat higher, for example in connection with the f4-f5 breakthrough. Even so, Black should have settled for this endgame, in order to avoid White's attacking possibilities on move 7 in the game. } ) 
6. Ra5 { Heading for the seventh rank. } Bh6 $2 ( { A tactical mistake on this occasion. More tenacious was } 6... Qc2 7. Qf4 Qf5 ( { The desirable } 7... Qc7 { runs into } 8. Qa4 $1 { followed by a timely Ra7. } ) 
8. Qxf5 gxf5 { . Although this fortress can probably be taken if White plays f3-f4 and places the rook on the 4th rank, and finally invades with the king. Contrary to the game, here Black's bishop can't attack the h-pawn. } ) 
7. Qd4 $2 ( { In time-trouble both players missed } 7. Qa7 Kg7 8. Qe7 $1 { when the dual threats of 9.Ra7 and 9.Qf6+ cannot be parried properly; e.g. } Qc8 9. Ra7 Qf8 10. Qxe6 { with an easy win. } ) 
Qc1 8. h4 $6 { Egin aims at preventing an annoying queen check on g5, but from now on the h4-pawn turns into a target for the bishop. } ( { Nevertheless, it was not obvious how to make progress with White; e.g. } 8. Qd8 Qf4 9. Ra8 Bg7 { and Black holds. } ) 
Qf4 $1 { Supposedly we shouldn't exchange pieces when behind in material, but I am confident about my fortress idea. } 9. Ra4 ( { If } 9. Qe4 Kg7 10. Ra7 { then } g5 $5 { and Black should be okay; e.g. } 11. Qxf4 ( { after } 11. hxg5 $6 Qxg5 { the black queen is quite annoying; } ) 
gxf4 12. Kh3 ( { or } 12. Kf1 Kg6 13. Ke2 Bg7 14. Ra5 Kf5 { etc. } ) 
Kg6 13. Ra1 Bg7 14. Rg1 Kh6 { when neither } 15. Rg5 ( { nor } 15. Re1 Kg6 ) 
Bf8 { leads anywhere. } ) 
Qxd4 10. Rxd4 { In this endgame White has two main ideas: a) place the rook on the seventh rank and transfer the king to e8 so as to attack the f7-pawn; b) sacrifice a pawn with f3-f4-f5 in order to weaken the h5-pawn (in the event of ...g6xf5) or enable an invasion with the king by Kd5 and e5-e6 (if Black replies ...e6xf5). } Bf8 { It is time to use the bishop for interfering purposes. } 11. Rd7 Kg7 12. Kg3 Bc5 13. f4 ( { If } 13. Rd8 { denying the bishop the h4-d8 diagonal, Black could reply } Be7 14. Re8 Bc5 15. f4 Bb6 { when White can't easily activate his king without dropping a pawn; e.g. } 16. Kf3 Bc5 17. Ke2 Bb6 18. f3 Bc5 19. Kd3 Bf2 { while if the rook leaves e8, then the bishop can return to e7. } ) 
Kf8 14. Rb7 Ke8 15. f3 Be7 { The bishop's preferred diagonal, where it hits the most valuable of the enemy pawns. } ( { In contrast, after } 15... Bf8 $6 16. Rb8 Ke7 17. Kf2 Bh6 { White could try } 18. Ke3 Bg7 19. Ke4 Bh6 20. Ra8 Bg7 21. f5 $1 { in accordance with plan 'b' outlined above; e.g. } gxf5 ( { or } 21... exf5 22. Kd5 { preparing Ra7+ and e5-e6 } ) 
22. Kd4 { and the h5-pawn is in trouble. } ) 
16. Kh3 Bd8 17. Ra7 Be7 18. Kg3 Bd8 19. Ra4 Be7 20. Rd4 Bd8 21. Rd1 Be7 22. Rh1 Bc5 { Simplest, in order to restrict the enemy king. } 23. Rc1 ( { If } 23. Kg2 { then } Be3 $1 ) 
Be7 24. Rc4 Bd8 25. Kg2 Be7 ( { Of course not } 25... Bxh4 $2 26. f5 -- { followed by } 27. f6 { , and the f7-pawn becomes a problem. } ) 
26. Kg3 ( { More critical was } 26. Kf1 { trying to activate the king. I was planning to reply } f5 $5 { in order to win the h4-pawn, although after } 27. exf6 Bxf6 28. Re4 $1 Kf7 29. Ke2 { a waiting game with } Be7 { is preferable ; e. g. } ( { Black should avoid } 29. Kg3 Bxh4 $2 30. f5 { when White obtains a strong passed pawn } ) 
30. Kd3 Bf6 31. Kc4 Be7 32. Kb5 Kf6 $1 { (heading for f5) } 33. Re5 Bd6 ( { or } 33. Kg3 Kf7 { and a draw is inevitable. } ) 
 ) 
Bd8 27. Ra4 Be7 28. Ra8+ Kd7 29. Ra7+ Ke8 30. Rb7 Bd8 31. Rb8 ( { In the event of } 31. f5 { the simplest reply is } exf5 { when } 32. e6 $6 fxe6 33. Rg7 Bf6 34. Rxg6 $4 Kf7 35. Rh6 Kg7 36. Rxh5 Kg6 { ends in disaster for White. In any case, it is evident that the f4-f5 advance becomes less powerful when the white king is not actively placed. } ) 
Kd7 32. Ra8 Be7 33. Ra1 Ke8 34. Rh1 Bc5 35. Rh2 Bb6 36. Rh1 Bc5 37. Kg2 Be3 38. Kg3 Bc5 
