 ( { White's position seems difficult in view of Black's advanced passed pawns. The immediate threat is } 1... d2 { , followed by } 2. -- Kd7 { , liberating the rook from the defence of the knight. Then ideas like ...Rc1+ and ...d1Q would be in the air. } ) 
1. { -KEY- } Nxc2 $1 { At first sight this looks like a miscalculation, but it is actually White's best option. } ( { The natural } 1. Kf1 $6 { could be met by } Kd7 2. Rb5 Kc7 3. Nd5 Kd6 4. e5 ( { or } 4. Nxb4 $2 Nxb4 5. Rxb4 Rc1 6. Kf2 Kc5 { and the threat of 7...d2 can't be parried; e.g. } 7. Rb2 Rc2 ) 
Ke6 5. Ne3 Re2 { and White remains in difficulties. } ) 
dxc2 2. Rxc6 b3 { Evidently, one of the passed pawns will soon queen. But the fact that the further battle will be reduced to one single flank, alongside the secure location of White's king, protecting (and protected by!) his fellow pawns, makes the thought of a fortress quite realistic. Incidentally, there exist many fortresses within queen vs. rook and pawn(s) endgames - for example, Kg1, Rg3 and Pf2 vs. Kf6 and Qe5 is a draw. } 3. Kh2 b2 { The further course of the game will show us that, once White freezes the pawn structure by e4-e5, his defensive task is made easier. } ( { Thus } 3... f6 $5 { was an interesting alternative, upon which White should probably reply } 4. e5 $1 { anyway, getting rid of a future weakness. There could follow } fxe5 ( { after } 4... f5 $6 5. Rc3 b2 6. Rxc2 b1=Q $11 7. Rf2 Kf7 8. Rf3 { we have reached the first of several fortresses within this example, one that Black is unlikely to destroy, e.g. } g5 9. fxg5 Kg6 10. Rf4 Qe1 11. Rh4 Qxe5 12. Rf4 Kxg5 13. Rh4 { and the rook keeps alternating between f4 and h4; } ) 
 ( { while the fortress arising after } 4... b2 5. Rxc2 b1=Q $11 6. Re2 { seems just as resistant, e.g. } Qd3 7. Re1 Qd2 8. Re4 Kf7 9. exf6 Kxf6 10. Re5 { and this time the rook alternates between g5 and e5; } ) 
5. fxe5 Kd7 6. Rc3 Ke6 ( { or } 6... b2 7. Rxc2 b1=Q $11 8. Rf2 $1 { with similar play; } ) 
7. Rxb3 c1=Q $11 { In order for White's fortress to work, he must deploy his rook in such a way that Black won't be able to approach the white g-pawns with his king, nor create zugzwang. For this purpose it makes sense to use the f-file: } 8. Rf3 $1 Kxe5 9. Rf4 g5 { (otherwise the rook keeps alternating between f3 and f4) } 10. Rf3 Ke4 11. Rf7 ( 10... b2 10. Rb3 { seems just as possible; e.g. } Qd1 12. Rf3 Qd2 13. Ra3 g4 14. Ra4 { followed by Rf4; } ) 
Ke3 12. Rf3 Ke2 13. Rf7 Qc5 14. Rf3 Qe7 ( { here and later, } 14... g4 15. Rf4 { when the safe square on f3 is replaced with f4; } ) 
15. Kg1 $1 Qa7 16. Kh2 { and as far as I can see, Black is unable to progress. By the way, note that White's extra pawn rules out a queen sacrifice for the rook; e.g. 9...Qxf4! would have been winning were it not for the g2-pawn. } ) 
4. Rc8+ $1 { By driving the enemy king away, Sokolov facilitates the e4-e5 advance, something that will be more evident on move 7. } ( { Less accurate is } 4. Rxc2 $6 b1=Q $11 5. Re2 ( { or } 5. Rc4 Qb5 ) 
Qd3 6. Re1 Ke7 { and Black should win - Vaganian. } ) 
Ke7 ( { After } 4... Kd7 { , Black's king is exposed to a vertical check, which yields White the necessary tempo to realize e4-e5; i.e. } 5. Rxc2 b1=Q $11 6. Rd2 $1 { followed by 7.e5 and the fortress is complete - Vaganian. } ) 
5. Rc7+ Kf8 ( { Or } 5... Kf6 6. Rxc2 b1=Q $11 7. e5 Kf5 8. Rf2 { with the same fortress that later emerges in the game. } ) 
6. Rxc2 b1=Q 7. Rc8+ $1 { With Black unable to reply 7...Kd7, White wins a tempo for the realization of e4-e5. } Ke7 8. e5 Qd3 9. Rc6 { Temporarily restricting the enemy king. } ( 9. Ra8 { was possible too, heading for the f-file; e.g. } Ke6 10. Ra2 Qe3 { (preventing Rf2-f3) } 11. Ra1 Qe2 12. Ra3 $1 -- { followed by } 13. Rf3 ) 
Qe3 10. Ra6 { Sokolov performs the rook transfer mentioned in the previous note. } Qc5 11. Ra1 Ke6 12. Rf1 Kd5 13. Rf3 { The defensive plan that was initialized by the first diagram is finally complete. White's next task is to maintain the fortress. } Kc4 14. Kh1 Qd4 15. Kh2 Qd1 16. Rf2 Kd4 17. Rf3 Ke4 18. Rc3 ( { a) Another option is } 18. Rf2 Ke3 19. Rf3 Ke2 20. Rc3 { when the rook has a sufficient number of squares on the third rank. If Black's king moves to f2 or f1, it will be expelled by Rf3+. } ) 
 ( { b) In contrast, } 18. Ra3 $6 { runs into } f6 $1 { complicating White's defence, now that } 19. exf6 $2 { fails to } Qh5 20. Kg1 Qc5 { - Vaganian. } ) 
